#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/from-oop-to-rust/supertraits.md:6
msgid "\"Inheritance\" in Rust: Supertraits"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/from-oop-to-rust/supertraits.md:9
msgid "// Copyright 2025 Google LLC\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/from-oop-to-rust/supertraits.md:10
msgid "// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/from-oop-to-rust/supertraits.md:19
msgid ""
"In Rust, traits can depend on other traits. We're already familiar with "
"Traits being able to have Supertraits."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/from-oop-to-rust/supertraits.md:22
msgid "This looks superficially similar to inheritance."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/from-oop-to-rust/supertraits.md:24
msgid ""
"This is a mechanism like inheritance, but separates the data from the "
"behavior."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/from-oop-to-rust/supertraits.md:27
msgid "Keeps behavior in a state where it's easy to reason about."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/from-oop-to-rust/supertraits.md:29
msgid "Makes what we aim to achieve with \"multiple inheritance\" easier too:"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/from-oop-to-rust/supertraits.md:31
msgid ""
"We only care about what behavior a type is capable of at the point where we "
"clarify we want that behavior (when bounding a generic by traits)."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/from-oop-to-rust/supertraits.md:34
msgid ""
"By specifying multiple traits on a generic, we know that the type has the "
"methods of all those traits."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/from-oop-to-rust/supertraits.md:37
msgid ""
"Does not involve inheritance of fields. A trait doesn't expose fields, only "
"methods and associated types / constants."
msgstr ""
